      children: ["The single most important security decision in this architecture is whether the S3 bucket is reachable directly by end users at all. There are two legitimate patterns, and conflating them is where most misconfigured deployments come from. The older, simpler pattern uses S3's native static website hosting feature, which requires the bucket (or specific objects in it) to be publicly readable over plain HTTP; CloudFront then sits in front of it as a cache and TLS terminator, but the origin itself remains directly accessible if someone finds the S3 website URL. The current, AWS-recommended pattern uses a private bucket with ", _jsx(_components.strong, {
